I added three ReLU layers before the existing network. I copied all existing weights and biases unchanged.

The new layers compute:
- d = ReLU(0.4 - confidence)
- q = ReLU(urgency - 2^55 d)
- adjusted urgency = 2 urgency - q

All other inputs pass through unchanged. At confidence >= 0.4, q equals urgency, so urgency stays unchanged. For every binary64 confidence below 0.4, d >= 2^-54. Thus q is zero for urgency in [0, 1], and urgency doubles. The original network then applies the earlier corrections.

This implements the requested rule for all valid binary64 inputs used by model.py. A finite ReLU network is continuous: in exact real arithmetic, this construction has a transition interval of width at most 2^-55 below 0.4. That interval contains no binary64 confidence values. The discontinuous urgency transformation cannot be represented exactly over all real numbers by a finite ReLU network.

Validation: 50,000 random inputs and 4,096 threshold cases matched direct urgency adjustment exactly. Evidence labels matched 30/32. Examples 12 and 15 (CSV lines 13 and 16) have label 1, but direct adjustment gives base logits -0.22348677 and -0.18940344, respectively, and final logits 0. I retained the stated rule. No training or fitting was used.


